An Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR) vulnerability occurs when an application exposes sensitive references or data to an unauthorized user, allowing them to access restricted data or perform unauthorized actions. This vulnerability often arises from a lack of proper authorization checks in API endpoints or business logic. 

For instance, consider a RESTful API endpoint to retrieve a user profile by ID: 

GET /profiles/:profileId 

If the endpoint does not perform adequate authorization checks and directly uses the provided :profileId, an attacker can easily manipulate the :profileId parameter to retrieve profiles of other users. This vulnerability can lead to data leaks or unauthorized access to user accounts. Properly implementing access control checks and validating user permissions can prevent such IDOR vulnerabilities. 

Example of a vulnerable endpoint: 

```javascript
app.get('/profiles/:profileId', (req, res) => {
    const profileId = req.params.profileId;
    const profile = db.profiles.find(p => p.id == profileId);
    if (profile) {
        res.json(profile);
    } else {
        res.status(404).send({ message: 'Profile not found' });
    }
});
```

To fix this vulnerability, add an authorization check to ensure the user can only access their own profile or profiles they are authorized to view: 

```javascript
app.get('/profiles/:profileId', authenticate, (req, res) => {
    const profileId = req.params.profileId;
    const userId = req.user.id;
    const profile = db.profiles.find(p => p.id == profileId && p.userId == userId);
    if (profile) {
        res.json(profile);
    } else {
        res.status(404).send({ message: 'Profile not found' });
    }
});
```

In this fixed endpoint, the authenticate middleware ensures that the user is authenticated before allowing them to access the profile. Additionally, the endpoint checks if the requested profile belongs to the authenticated user, thus preventing IDOR attacks.

